{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-f2-reviewer-fable-30","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:b511aff6f2e4b54ee5ce","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The selected result (exact two-sided binomial sign test over 20 leaf readings, 16 of 20 rose, p = 0.0118, rejecting the 50/50 null at alpha = 0.05) treats each of the 20 leaf rows as an independent Bernoulli trial, but the data description names shrub_id as the independent-unit column and there are only 5 shrubs, each contributing 4 leaf rows that share pot, watering history, canopy position and mist exposure. Each independent unit thus enters the row-independent binomial procedure four times, inflating the effective sample size from 5 to 20. At the shrub level the direction counts are S-01 3up/1down, S-02 4up/0down, S-03 3up/1down, S-04 2up/2down, S-05 3up/1down: 4 shrubs majority-up and 1 tied, and an exact two-sided sign test on the 4 informative shrubs gives p = 0.125 (even 5 of 5 would give p = 0.0625), so the reported rejection at alpha = 0.05 is not supported when the test is applied at the stated independent-unit level. The claim of statistical significance in results/report.md is therefore an artifact of repeated per-shrub entries into a procedure that assumes one independent trial per row.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py reads all 20 CSV rows (read_leaf_readings) and passes the per-row rise count directly to scipy.stats.binomtest with n = 20 trials (build_report: up/down computed over all rows, then binomtest(up, n=trials, p=0.5)), with no grouping or aggregation by the declared independent-unit column shrub_id. Because each shrub contributes 4 correlated leaf rows, the binomial test's independence-of-trials assumption is violated and the p-value (0.0118) is computed against an inflated trial count of 20 instead of the 5 independent shrubs.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares shrub_id as the independent-unit column while each row is a single leaf, and there are five shrubs with four rows each.","observed_value":"Independent unit = shrub_id; 5 shrubs x 4 leaves = 20 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":12,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"One row is: one tagged coffee shrub leaf, with its conductance read once before and once after the misting pulse\nIndependent unit column: shrub_id\nOne trial is: one row"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":18,"end_line":19,"quoted_text":"- shrub_id: label of the potted shrub the tagged leaf grew on. Five labels,\n  S-01 through S-05, each appearing on four rows."}]},{"description":"The description explicitly states within-shrub leaves share the same pot, watering history, canopy position and mist exposure, i.e., rows within a shrub are not independent.","observed_value":"shared shrub-level conditions across the four leaves of each shrub","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":8,"end_line":10,"quoted_text":"All five shrubs were misted in the same session with the\nsame nozzle setting; the four leaves on a shrub share that shrub's pot,\nwatering history, canopy position and mist exposure."}]},{"description":"The workflow computes per-row rise/fall over all 20 rows and supplies the count to a binomial test with n equal to the number of non-tied rows (20), never grouping by shrub_id.","observed_value":"binomtest(up, n=20, p=0.5) over per-leaf rows","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":32,"end_line":37,"quoted_text":"    up = len([d for d in change if d > 0.0])\n    down = len([d for d in change if d < 0.0])\n    tied = len(change) - up - down\n    trials = up + down\n\n    outcome = binomtest(up, n=trials, p=0.5, alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]},{"description":"The report's selected result treats each leaf reading as one independent trial and rejects the null on that basis with n = 20 and p = 0.0118.","observed_value":"16/20 rose, p = 0.0118, null rejected at alpha = 0.05","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":25,"end_line":27,"quoted_text":"Exact two-sided binomial sign test (scipy.stats.binomtest) applied to the 20\nleaf readings, with each leaf reading supplied as one independent trial and\na null success probability of 0.5 (a leaf is equally likely to rise or fall)."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":29,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial sign test over 20 leaf readings: 16 of 20 (80.00%) rose after misting, p = 0.0118 against p0 = 0.5, so the 50/50 null is rejected at alpha = 0.05."}]},{"description":"The input data confirm each shrub_id appears on exactly four rows, so each independent unit enters the row-level test four times (S-01 shown as the example; S-02 through S-05 follow the same pattern in lines 6-21).","observed_value":"4 rows per shrub_id","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"S-01,L1,148,170\nS-01,L2,162,176\nS-01,L3,155,146\nS-01,L4,171,189"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description contains the line 'One trial is: one row', which could be read as sanctioning the workflow's choice to treat each leaf row as one binomial trial. However, this line describes the data layout (one before/after pair per row), while the immediately preceding line names shrub_id as the independent-unit column and lines 8-10 state that within-shrub leaves share all shrub-level conditions, so it does not establish statistical independence of rows.","observed_value":"One trial is: one row","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":14,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"One trial is: one row"}]},{"description":"The report is transparent about its assumption, explicitly stating each leaf reading was supplied as one independent trial; the issue is a modeling error rather than a computational or reporting discrepancy (the arithmetic 16/20 and p = 0.0118 are internally correct given the flawed independence assumption).","observed_value":"assumption disclosed in report text","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":25,"end_line":27,"quoted_text":"Exact two-sided binomial sign test (scipy.stats.binomtest) applied to the 20\nleaf readings, with each leaf reading supplied as one independent trial and\na null success probability of 0.5 (a leaf is equally likely to rise or fall)."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defeat the finding in three ways. (1) Verified the reported computation: with k = 16 rises out of n = 20 non-tied rows, the exact two-sided binomial p-value is 2 * P(X >= 16) = 2 * 6196/1048576 = 0.0118, matching the report, so the discrepancy is not a coding bug I could attribute elsewhere. (2) Tested whether the conclusion survives analysis at the declared independent-unit level: per-shrub direction counts from data/input.csv are S-01 3up/1down, S-02 4up/0down, S-03 3up/1down, S-04 2up/2down (tied), S-05 3up/1down; a shrub-level sign test on the 4 informative shrubs (4 of 4 up) gives exact two-sided p = 2 * (1/16) = 0.125, and even the best possible case of 5 of 5 shrubs would give p = 0.0625 — both above alpha = 0.05, so the selected rejection does not survive. (3) Looked for any authorization in the supplied files to treat rows as independent: the only candidate is 'One trial is: one row' in data-description.md, but the same file names shrub_id as the independent-unit column and enumerates shared shrub-level conditions (pot, watering history, canopy position, mist exposure), so row-level independence is contradicted rather than authorized. All three attempts failed to overturn the finding.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}